\title{msamodel: Mutation-Stability-Activity Model of Protein Structural Evolution}
\description{
Implementation of the Mutation-Stability-Activity (MSA) model of protein structural evolution, in which mutations perturb the structure and become fixed or are lost depending on their effects on stability and activity, weighted by stability and activity selection strengths, respectively. Given a single structure and its active-site residues, predicts structural divergence profiles in two representations, per residue and per normal mode. The two selection strengths can be set to chosen values or estimated from observed profiles by maximum likelihood, with delta-method standard errors. Predicted profiles can be analysed in terms of nested models in which one or both selection constraints are dropped, and decomposed as a sum of mutation, stability, and activity contributions.
}
\details{
In the MSA model, mutations perturb the structure and become fixed or are lost
depending on their effects on stability and activity. Given a structure and a
list of its active-site residues, the model predicts structural divergence
profiles.

Every profile comes in two representations, the same divergence written in two
bases: per residue, and per normal mode. Every profile function returns both at
once, as a list with a \code{$site} and a \code{$mode} tibble.

Two selection strengths govern the model: \code{a1} (selection on stability) and \code{a2}
(selection on activity, i.e. proximity to the active site). Both are
non-negative, and \code{0} switches that pressure off. They are either set to chosen
values, or estimated from an observed divergence profile.
}
\section{The workflow}{


\describe{
\item{1. Build the elastic network}{\code{\link[penm:set_enm]{penm::set_enm()}}, which belongs to the penm
package, not to msamodel.}
\item{2. Run the mutation scan}{\code{\link[=generate_spm]{generate_spm()}} mutates every site many times
and records how the structure responds. Returns the \code{spm} object every later
function reads.}
\item{3a. Evaluate at chosen \verb{(a1, a2)}}{\code{\link[=calculate_profiles]{calculate_profiles()}} for the
divergence profiles, \code{\link[=calculate_decomposition]{calculate_decomposition()}} for the four nested models
and the mutation/stability/activity split.}
\item{3b. Or estimate \verb{(a1, a2)} from data}{\code{\link[=fit_lrmsd_msa_site]{fit_lrmsd_msa_site()}} fits an
observed site profile, \code{\link[=fit_lrmsd_msa_mode]{fit_lrmsd_msa_mode()}} an observed mode profile. Both
return the estimate, its standard errors, and a goodness-of-fit summary in
\code{$gof}.}
\item{4. Propagate the fit}{\code{\link[=predict_profiles]{predict_profiles()}} and \code{\link[=predict_decomposition]{predict_decomposition()}}
give the same quantities as step 3a, with delta-method standard errors
carried through from the fit.}
}

\code{\link[=pfix_msa]{pfix_msa()}} exposes the model's elementary quantity, a single mutant's fixation
probability.
}

\section{Two metrics}{


Every profile function takes a \code{metric} argument. \code{"lrmsd"} is the profile as the
model predicts it, keeping its overall level; \code{"nlrmsd"} is the same profile with
its mean subtracted off. An observed divergence profile pins down shape but not
level, so \code{"nlrmsd"} is the one to compare against data.
}

